n0.text:
{vessel} {crashed|went down|got smashed} on {ground}, {broadcasting a dead distress signal|bleeding static into the comms|running a busted beacon} every {few|many} seconds.
{The airlock is popped, the reactor is dead cold, but something inside is still drawing power.|Reactor's dead cold, life support is gone, but some idiot left the beacon on.|The hull is breached and freezing, but internal power is still humming.}
{Either it's automated junk or a very obvious trap.|Looks like free salvage, if you don't mind the radiation.|Appears to be free salvage, if you don't mind the radiation.|Seems like free salvage, if you don't mind the radiation.|Clearly free salvage, if you don't mind the radiation.|Nobody came to help them, but we might as well loot the corpse.}
The beacon is not running on the ship's power. It has been {rewired|moved|taken off the bus} and given a cell of its own.

{Zero solid ground anywhere.|No landing zones in sight.|Nothing but thick, churning atmospheric layers.} The signal comes from a fixed depth in {place}, {anchored dead still in an atmosphere that should crush steel|holding station against all logic|hovering in the middle of a toxic cloud bank}.
{It's either an automated relay station or someone very stubborn.|You're going to have to dive right into the soup to check it out.|Descending into that soup is definitely going to void the hull warranty.}
It has been at that exact depth for as long as the survey has records, through {four|five} storm seasons that should have moved it.

{A bell is clanging out in the middle of nowhere, {distance}, where the charts swear there's only deep water.
Through the mist you spot a rusted buoy bobbing awkwardly in the swell. Someone welded an iron ladder onto the side and literally spray-painted a fake door with a real handle onto the metal.|There's a buoy out here in two hundred meters of open water, ringing like crazy. Up close, it's a beat-up rusted drum with a welded ladder and a painted door that shouldn't be there.|There's a buoy out here in four hundred meters of open water, ringing like crazy. Up close, it's a beat-up rusted drum with a welded ladder and a painted door that shouldn't be there.}
The bell is rung by the swell and the swell is not big enough to ring it.

{A glowing translucent figure in a tweed waistcoat is floating two feet off the ground in the ruined courtyard.|There's an actual ghost hovering over the rubble, clutching a glowing grimorie and muttering to himself.|A spectral clerk is pacing the cracked marble floor, furiously stamping imaginary papers.}
{He looks up, adjusts ghostly spectacles, and demands to see your permits.|He spots the party and immediately starts listing municipal infractions from two centuries ago.|He yells that visiting hours ended in 1842 and threatens to fine your ancestors.}
The summons in its hand is {countersigned|stamped twice|endorsed}, and one of the two hands that signed it is still working.

{An overgrown cemetery on the hill, packed with leaning headstones and aggressively judgmental stone angels.|A dreary graveyard where the dead have better real estate than the living.|Weeds and ivy are choking a massive marble tomb in the center of the graveyard.}
{The iron gate to the main mausoleum is slightly ajar, with fresh footprints in the moss.|Someone clearly tried to pry the heavy brass door handles off with a crowbar.|Sensors detect high magical resonance and zero signs of respect for the deceased.}
The crater is {not new|weathered|a year old at least} and nothing has grown in it.

{Sitting on a dusty shelf in the vestry is a butchered Anoki handset with its casing cracked open.|There's an Anoki phone sitting in the back of the vestry, held together with electrical tape.|On a stone ledge behind the altar sits an Anoki phone wired directly into the wall.} {The leyline coil has been ripped out and re-soldered by an amateur.|Someone clearly jury-rigged the charging circuit into the stone masonry.|The battery was removed years ago, but the screen is still glowing.}
{It starts ringing-not with the standard digital chime, but an annoying, cracked mechanical buzz from inside the plastic.|Suddenly the damn thing starts buzzing with a tinny, rusted bell chime.|Right on cue, it rings with a harsh, broken rattle that makes your teeth ache.}
The solder on the wires is {fresh|bright|not old} and the handset has been in the vestry since {timeAgo}.

c.text:
{The phone buzzes constantly for forty minutes while you tear the vestry apart looking for loot.|You ignore the persistent ringing and systematically rifle through every drawer and cupboard.}
{Jackpot in the back closet: an encrypted grimorie of phone numbers, a bottle of vintage wine, and a sealed lockbox.|You find four candles, an unopened bottle of communion liquor, and a stack of valuable notes.}
{At least the annoying racket distracted anyone from noticing you ransack the place.|Free loot and vintage booze. Worth the headache.}

d.text:
{You chuck the phone into the baptismal font with a loud splash. The ringing cuts out instantly and the room temperature drops twenty degrees.|You drown the noisy handset in holy water; it sizzles, steams, and goes dead silent.}
{The screen is still lit under the water, stubbornly ticking up the call duration in neon green.|Through the murky water, the screen shows the call is still connected and racking up long-distance fees.}
{Vatican authorities will probably send a commendation for destroying unsanctioned tech, but your fingers are numb from the frost.|The parish will be thrilled you exorcised their phone, even if you ruined their holy water.}

a1.text:
{You keep them talking for forty minutes while they ramble about historical gossip, rationing, and an impending war.|They spend half an hour venting about grocery prices and political drama from decades ago.}
{In the middle of the rant, they casually drop coordinates and dates for an undiscovered military cache.|Right before hanging up, they let slip the location of a forgotten emergency vault.|Jackpot: they gave away the location of an old treasury drop.}
{The line goes dead with a loud pop, and the handset finally dies for good.|The phone clicks, sparks once, and burns out the antenna.|The connection cuts out, leaving you with valuable intel and a fried phone.}

a2.text:
{You give them the current year and date. There's a long, stunned silence, followed by a defeated groan on the line.|You break the news about what year it is. They curse loudly at the temporal delay.}
{They ask you to deliver a final message to their old partner who's now living in a retirement village.|They beg you to pass a message to an elderly contact nearby.}
{You look up the address, deliver the news, and pocket a surprisingly generous finder's fee.|A quick errand, a few awkward questions, and an easy moral victory.} {Everyone feels slightly less cynical for about ten minutes.|Not a bad haul for playing temporal receptionist.}

b1.text:
{You dial the second number and an old man answers on the first ring: "So someone finally found the vestry rig."|An elderly guy picks up immediately, asking if you're calling from the church.}
{Turns out he's a retired engineer in a care home in {region}, desperate to get his old prototype back before he kicks the bucket.|He built the hacked handset fifty years ago and offers his entire pension savings if you bring it to him.}
{Easy money. You pack the cursed phone into your bag and collect the bounty.|He pays up in clean cash without asking too many questions.}

n0.text:
{In the damp basement beneath the castle, a heavy iron vault door is sealed with three wax crests and an oversized padlock.|Down in the castle cellar sits an ancient iron door covered in peeling heraldic wax.|There's a reinforced vault door down here that looks like it hasn't been opened since the monarchy collapsed.}
{The hinges are rusted solid, but someone recently scratched a fresh line of warding runes across the threshold.|The lock has been picked twice, failed once, and jammed with lead solder.|Sensors show high-value loot inside and a fifty percent chance of ancient curses.}
Three crests, and the third one is {not a crest anybody can place|from an office that never existed|newer than the other two}.

{Deep in the swamp, an old wooden shack sits on rotting stilts, surrounded by bubbling mud and swarms of biting gnats.|A miserable shack is balanced over the swamp water, smelling of sulfur and cheap tobacco.|Out in the muck, a hermit's cabin is tied to three mangrove roots with frayed rope.}
{An old guy in rubber waders is sitting on the porch, cleaning a shotgun and eyeing your wallet.|A shifty swamp drifter is watching you through a cracked window, pretending to be deaf.|The local hermit waves you over with a rusty machete, looking for a sucker to trade with.}
The shack is a lid. The stilts are not holding it up, they are {holding it down|anchoring it|stopping it going anywhere}.

{vessel} sits at {place} with the ramp down and {cargo} half unloaded onto {ground}.
Nobody is aboard. The manifest is on a clip by the ramp, filled in properly all the way to the third page, and then not at all.
The third page ends mid-word. {Not mid-line: mid-word.|The pen stopped inside a word.|Somebody stopped writing between one letter and the next.}
